{
  "result": "Every morning, the baker opened his shop before the sun had decided what kind of day it wanted to be. He swept the floor, lit the ovens, and set yesterday\u2019s unsold bread on a wooden bench outside. A handwritten sign beside it read: Take what you need; leave what you can.\n\nAt first, people left coins. Then someone left a jar of jam. A child contributed a drawing of a dog with seven legs. An old woman brought tomatoes from her garden, still warm from the afternoon light. Before long, the bench became less like a shelf and more like a conversation.\n\nNobody organized it. That was perhaps why it worked. A person who needed a loaf might also have a spare book. Someone who took the book might return with a packet of seeds. The gifts were rarely equal in price, but nobody seemed interested in balancing the account.\n\nOne winter, a storm knocked out the power across town. The baker could not use his ovens, so he worried the bench would stand empty. Instead, neighbors filled it with candles, blankets, thermoses of soup, and notes offering rides. By evening, strangers were sharing stories under the awning.\n\nWhen the lights returned, the baker found a note tucked beneath an empty soup pot. It said, We thought you should know the bread was never really the point. He folded the paper, slipped it into his apron, and went inside to prepare dough for the next beautifully uncertain, unexpectedly generous morning.",
